2. Referenced, not included
---------------------------

These are loaded from the system at run time and are not part of any archive
distributed here.

On Windows, kernel32.dll is imported statically by every Go binary, cgo or not:
the runtime needs it for memory, threads and exception handling. It is the only
entry in the import table. The runtime also resolves ntdll.dll and
bcryptprimitives.dll by name at run time, and the engine itself does the same
for user32.dll and gdi32.dll — the window and the presentation — and for
winmm.dll — audio. All of those are Windows components, shipped with the
operating system and absent from any archive distributed here.

On Linux, the engine speaks the X11 protocol over a Unix socket and the audio
server's protocol over another. No client library is loaded: a binary built
without cgo has no interpreter and no DT_NEEDED entry, so nothing is referenced
beyond the kernel interface.

The interoperability target is the one exception to a build free of C. Produced
with CGO_ENABLED=1, it additionally references the platform's C runtime — the
Universal C Runtime on Windows, glibc and libgcc_s on Linux — which remains a
system component, not a part of this distribution.
